       "number": 1637,
       "title": "slots declared on attrs-decorated class are ignored/lost",
       "body": "If a slotted `attrs` class declares its own slots, those slots are lost/ignored when the class is processed by `attrs`. E.g.\n\n```python\n@attrs.define\nclass A:\n    __slots__ = (\"private\",)\n\n    public: str = \"foo\"\n\na = A()\n\na.private = 1\n```\n\nproduces the error `TypeError: descriptor 'private' for 'A' objects doesn't apply to a 'A' object`. \n\nHowever, declaring an `attrs` class that  subclasses a class that defines slots, the parent class's slots are preserved. E.g.\n\n```python\nclass Base:\n    __slots__ = (\"private\",)\n\n@attrs.define\nclass B(Base):\n    public: str = \"bar\"\n\nb = B()\n\nb.private = 1\n```\n\nsets the slots attribute without issue.\n\nIs this intentional behavior or dictated by the design",
       "state": "OPEN",
       "stateReason": null,
       "createdAt": "2026-10-02T23:19:05Z",
       "closedAt": null,
       "authorAssociation": "NONE",
       "author": {
